No, I don't think the example needs {co}.  It's a school type of library.
 We're talking about the library so it should be the last word of the tanru.

From jbovlaste:

        Word: ckusro
<http://www.lojban.org/cgi-bin/dict.pl?Form=dict.pl2&Database=*&Query=ckusro>
[jbovlaste] <http://www.lojban.org/jbovlaste/dict/ckusro>
        Type: lujvo
  Gloss Word: book collection
<http://www.lojban.org/cgi-bin/dict.pl?Form=dict.pl2&Database=*&Query=book%20collection>
  Gloss Word: library
<http://www.lojban.org/cgi-bin/dict.pl?Form=dict.pl2&Database=*&Query=library>
in the sense of "collection of books"
  Definition: s1 is a library of books c1.

What you're trying to say is that the library does contain a
particular book right?  So it makes sense that you could just say that
it is not a (library with bookX).  That was the basis for {na'e ckusro
be la brodas}.


I like Pierre's {claxu} idea better though.


